`podchecker` on the three modules of this distribution:

    lib/Git/Libgit2.pm        has 14 pod syntax errors
    lib/Git/Libgit2/FFI.pm    has 241 pod syntax errors
    lib/Git/Libgit2/Error.pm  has 8 pod syntax errors

Two separate causes.

**1. The house directives are not POD.** The files use their own directives, which
Pod::Checker rejects as unknown:

    =func        246 x   (FFI.pm, Git::Libgit2.pm)
    =method        5 x   (Error.pm)
    =synopsis      3 x
    =description   3 x
    =seealso       1 x

That is 258 of the 263 errors. The convention is used elsewhere in the house as well
(karr, io-k8s-p5, citiai-core show the same podchecker output), so the fix is a house
decision, not a local one: either map the directives onto standard POD (`=head2` /
`=head3`), or declare them to Pod::Checker.

A side effect worth knowing: because `L</init_lib>` is resolved against headings only,
the custom directive breaks internal links too - `Git::Libgit2.pm:269` and `:270` point
at `init_lib`, which is documented under `=func init_lib` (`Git::Libgit2.pm:247`). Those
two "unresolved internal link" errors are the same root cause.

**2. No module carries `=encoding`.** All three files contain non-ASCII (the em dash
in "—"), and none has an `=encoding utf8` line. Pod::Checker answers for each file:

    Non-ASCII character seen before =encoding in '—'. Assuming UTF-8
    *** ERROR: Wide character in print at Pod/Checker.pm line 636.

Done when: the three modules pass `podchecker` without errors - either by switching to
standard POD directives or by making the custom ones known to the checker - and by
carrying an `=encoding utf8` line.

